Object detection has gained substantial popularity in recent times due to its
versatile applications. Its utilization spans across multiple domains, such as
enhancing security measures, enabling the functionality of self-driving cars,
monitoring transportation activities, analyzing drone footage, and
facilitating visual perception for robots [1].Recent technological
breakthroughs, particularly in advancing deep convolutional neural
networks (CNNs) and harnessing the impressive computational capabilities
of GPUs, have played a crucial role in advancing object detection. This

progress has found diverse applications in security, military, transportation,
medical, and everyday life fields. These innovations have not only exerted a
significant influence on but have also substantially elevated the
effectiveness of object detection methodologies.
This research delves into the application of YOLO (You Only Look Once)
for detecting a range of objects, both small and large, on the surfaces of
solar panels. Solar panels are recognized as a leading source of renewable
energy in the modern world, presenting a clean and efficient energy
solution. Nevertheless, a noteworthy challenge arises when the efficiency of
these panels decreases due to the accumulation of objects like dust particles,
sand, bird droppings, and even larger debris such as leaves on their top
surfaces. The efficiency of the solar panel experiences a reduction of 0.4%
with the deposition of 1 g/m 2 of dust on its surface, and a decrease of 0.15%
is observed for a dust deposition of 0.1 g/m 2 [2].
Our primary objective is to meticulously train, test, and validate diverse
methodologies, ensuring their effectiveness in accurately identifying
specific objects present on the solar panel&#39;s surface. In practical terms, the
significance of this study extends to real-world scenarios: when seamlessly
integrated with IoT robots, the identified objects can be systematically
addressed for cleaning. This process aims to enhance the overall efficiency
and performance of solar panels, contributing to the sustainable utilization
of solar energy.
